","Here let's break down each study type:

**a) To see if there is a relationship between smoking and bladder cancer, patients with bladder cancer are asked if they currently smoke or if they smoked in the past.**

*   **Retroactive:** This is a retrospective study. Researchers are looking backward, looking on past smoking history to see if it's associated with a current diagnosis of bladder cancer. They start with the outcome (bladder cancer) and look back to identify potential risk factors.

**b) The Nurses Health Survey was a survey where nurses were asked to record their eating habits over a period of time, and their general health was recorded.**

*   **Cross-sectional:** This is a prospective study. The study looks at data collected at a single point in time. It looks on eating habits and health at the same time.

**c) A new study is underway to track the eating and exercise patterns of people at different time-periods in the future, and see who is afflicted with cancer later in life.**

*   **Prospective:** This is a prospective study. Researchers start with a group of individuals, collect data on their eating and exercise habits, and then follow them over time to see who develops cancer. They look forward to observe the development of a disease.

**d) The prices of generic items are compared to the prices of the equivalent named brand items.**

*   **Cross-itudinal:** This is a cross-sectional study. It compares two groups (generic and branded items) at a single point in time. It doesn't look back to the past or look forward to future outcomes.",relevance,"# Task Description
