","Here solution:

(a) The electric force on the proton is given by $F = qE$, where $q$ is the charge of the proton and $E$ is the electric field strength.
$F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}$
The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second Law: $a = \frac{F}{m}$, where $m$ is the mass of the proton.
$a = \F/m = \frac{800 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} \approx 483 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2 = 483 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2$

(b) We can use the equation $v = u + a t$, where $v$ is the final velocity, $u$ is the initial velocity, $a$ is the acceleration, and $t$ is the time. Since the proton starts from rest, $u =0$.
$v = a t$
$t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{35000 \, \text{m/s}}{483 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2} \approx 7.35 \times 10^{-3} \, \text{s}$

